'''Michelle Lynn Lujan Grisham''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|l|uː|h|ɑː|n|_|ˈ|ɡ|r|ɪ|ʃ|əm}} {{respell|LOO|hahn|_|GRISH|əm}};<ref>{{cite AV media |date= Aug 23, 2022|title= Manny|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3CHVY-foAZU|publisher=Michelle Lujan Grisham|via=[[YouTube]]|access-date=August 20, 2024 |time= 00:24}}</ref> née '''Lujan'''; born October 24, 1959) is an American lawyer and politician serving as the 32nd [[governor of New Mexico]] since 2019. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], Lujan Grisham previously served as the [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] for [[New Mexico's 1st congressional district]] from 2013 to 2019.

Lujan Grisham served as the state [[New Mexico Department of Health|secretary of health]] from 2004 to 2007 and as a [[Bernalillo County, New Mexico|Bernalillo County]] [[County commission|commissioner]] from 2010 to 2012. She was elected to the U.S. House of Representatives in [[2012 United States House of Representatives elections in New Mexico|2012]], defeating [[Janice Arnold-Jones]]. In 2016, Lujan Grisham was selected as the chair of the [[Congressional Hispanic Caucus]]. She won the Democratic nomination for governor of New Mexico in [[2018 New Mexico gubernatorial election|2018]] and defeated [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Steve Pearce (politician)|Steve Pearce]] on November 6, 2018. She was reelected in [[2022 New Mexico gubernatorial election|2022]].

==Early life and education==
Michelle Lynn Lujan<ref>{{cite news |date=May 12, 1983 |title=Wedding: Lujan, grisham |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/albuquerque-journal-lujan-grisham/134099778/ |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]] |location=Albuquerque, NM |page=22 |via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> was born in [[Los Alamos, New Mexico]] and grew up in [[Santa Fe, New Mexico|Santa Fe]]. Her father, Llewellyn Eugene "Buddy" Lujan,<ref>{{cite tweet|number=1008383268528508928|title=Happy Father's Day, New Mexico! Not a day goes by that I don't miss my dad Llewellyn "Buddy" Lujan. I learned about helping others by watching him work -- everyone was welcome in his chair. I hope everyone enjoys some quality time with their loved ones today! #nmpol|user=Michelle4NM|date=2018-06-17}}</ref> was a dentist into his eighties until he died in March 2011. Her mother, Sonja Lee (née Jackson),<ref>{{Cite news|first=Susan Montoya |last=Bryan |title= Sonja Lujan, mother of NM governor, passes away at 82  |newspaper=[[KOB (TV)]]|date=April 11, 2022 |url= https://www.kob.com/archive/sonja-lujan-mother-of-nm-governor-passes-away-at-82/ |quote=Born Sonja Lee Jackson on Jan, 18, 1940, in Brazil, Indiana, she and her family lived abroad at times as her father's career in the U.S. Air Force landed them in Germany and Japan.}}</ref> was a homemaker originally from Indiana who died in 2022. Her sister Kimberly was diagnosed with a brain tumor at the age of two and died at 21.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2013/01/michelle-lujan-grisham-profile-86137.html |title=Sister's death drives Michelle Lujan Grisham |author=Seung Min Kim |publisher=[[Politico]] |date=January 28, 2013 |access-date=July 21, 2013}}</ref>

Lujan Grisham's [[Hispanos of New Mexico|Hispano]]<ref>{{Cite web |title= LUJAN GRISHAM, Michelle {{!}} US House of Representatives: History, Art & Archives |url= https://history.house.gov/People/Listing/L/LUJAN-GRISHAM,-Michelle-(L000580)/ |access-date= 2026-08-15 |website= history.house.gov |language= en}}</ref> ancestors have resided in New Mexico for 12 generations.<ref>{{Cite web| url= https://apnews.com/58ccd043a6ea47688bf0bb0afcc3e388| title= New Mexico elects Democrat Michelle Lujan Grisham governor| first= Morgan| last= Lee|website= [[Associated Press]] |date= November 7, 2018| agency= [[Associated Press|AP News]]}}</ref> In an interview, she said most of her family was from Spain's Basque region and "were likely Jewish and fleeing persecution".<ref>{{cite news |last1= Barghouty |first1= Leila |last2= Chakrabarti |first2= Meghna |title= How New Mexico's governor got big things done |url= https://www.wbur.org/onpoint/2026/06/08/new-mexico-governor-democrat |publisher= WBUR-FM |date= June 8, 2026}}</ref> She is part of the prominent [[List of United States political families (L)#Lujans|Lujan political family]] in New Mexico, many of whose members have served in elected and appointed positions in government.<ref>{{cite web| author= National Journal |url= https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2012/11/new-mexico-1st-house-district-michelle-lujan-grisham-d/429390/ |title= New Mexico, 1st House District: Michelle Lujan Grisham (D) |publisher= [[The Atlantic]] |date= November 6, 2012 |access-date= June 30, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.eenews.net/special_reports/energy_scions/stories/1060020946 |title= Newsmaker: In New Mexico, it's good to be a Luján| date= June 26, 2015 |publisher= www.eenews.net |access-date= June 30, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url= http://newmexicoindependent.com/71163/michelle-lujan-grisham-running-for-first-district-seat |title= Michelle Lujan Grisham running for First District seat |publisher= New Mexico Independent |access-date= September 14, 2012 |archive-date= October 13, 2012 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20121013233111/http://newmexicoindependent.com/71163/michelle-lujan-grisham-running-for-first-district-seat }}</ref>

Lujan graduated from [[St. Michael's High School]]. She received a Bachelor of Arts in university studies from the [[University of New Mexico]] in 1981, where she was a work-study student in the engineering department and was a member of the [[Delta Delta Delta]] sorority.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Michelle Lujan Grisham -|url=https://awpc.cattcenter.iastate.edu/directory/michelle-lujan-grisham/|access-date=2020-11-30|website=Archives of Women's Political Communication|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|first=Kent|last=Walz|title=Michelle Lujan Grisham: Energetic and 'all in'|url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1196753/michelle-lujan-grisham-energetic-and-all-in.html|access-date=2020-11-30|website=www.abqjournal.com|language=en-US}}</ref> In 1982, she married Gregory Alan Grisham. She also worked as a technical writing intern for the [[Westinghouse Electric Corporation]]. In 1987, Lujan Grisham earned a [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of New Mexico School of Law]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Harder |first=Amy |url=http://nationaljournal.com/congress-legacy/new-mexico-1st-house-district-20121105 |title=New Mexico, 1st House District |work=NationalJournal.com |access-date=July 21, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131102133803/http://www.nationaljournal.com/congress-legacy/new-mexico-1st-house-district-20121105 |archive-date=November 2, 2013 }}</ref><ref name=abq2007/>

==Early political career==
Lujan Grisham served as director of the New Mexico Aging and Long-Term Services Department under [[Bruce King]], [[Gary Johnson]], and [[Bill Richardson]]. During Richardson's tenure, the position was elevated to the state cabinet level. In 2004, he named Lujan Grisham as [[New Mexico Department of Health|New Mexico Secretary of Health]] and she served in the position until 2007.<ref name=abq2007>{{cite web|url=http://www.abqjournal.com/news/state/apgrisham10-11-07.htm |title=Former Health Secretary Grisham Announces for Congress |publisher=Abqjournal.com |date=October 11, 2007 |access-date=September 14, 2012}}</ref>

Lujan Grisham was later elected to the [[Bernalillo County, New Mexico|Bernalillo County]] [[County commission|Commission]], serving from 2010 to 2012.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=827161|title = Our Campaigns - Bernalillo County Commissioner 01 Race - Nov 02, 2010}}</ref>

==U.S. House of Representatives==

===Elections===

==== 2008 ====
{{See also|2008 United States House of Representatives elections in New Mexico#District 1}}
Lujan Grisham resigned as Secretary of Health in order to run for the [[United States House of Representatives]] in the 2008 elections, losing in the Democratic primary to [[Martin Heinrich]], who won with 44% of the vote. [[New Mexico Secretary of State]] [[Rebecca Vigil-Giron]] ranked second with 25% and Lujan Grisham ranked third with 24%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=365999 |title=NM District 1- D Primary Race – Jun 03, 2008 |publisher=Our Campaigns |access-date=July 21, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lcsun-news.com/las_cruces_election_2008/ci_9650380|title=Final Results: June 3 primary election|publisher=Las Cruces Sun-News|date=2008-06-20|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110524005445/http://www.lcsun-news.com/las_cruces_election_2008/ci_9650380 |archive-date=May 24, 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Salazar |first=Martin |url=http://www.abqjournal.com/news/metro/29113618metro06-29-08.htm |title=Top UNM official red-faced over Lujan Grisham comments |publisher=Abqjournal.com |date=June 29, 2008 |access-date=September 14, 2012}}</ref>

==== 2010 ====
Lujan Grisham was elected to the [[Bernalillo County, New Mexico|Bernalillo County]] [[County commission|Commission]], serving from 2010 to 2012.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=827161|title = Our Campaigns - Bernalillo County Commissioner 01 Race - Nov 02, 2010}}</ref> In August 2012, she resigned to focus on her congressional race.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2012-08-25 |title=Grisham steps down from commission |url=https://www.koat.com/article/grisham-steps-down-from-commission/5042661 |access-date=2025-02-18 |website=KOAT |language=en}}</ref>

==== 2012 ====
{{See also|2012 United States House of Representatives elections in New Mexico#District 1}}
Lujan Grisham sought the Democratic nomination for the House again in 2012 after Heinrich decided to run for the [[United States Senate]]. She won the nomination, defeating [[Marty Chavez]] and [[Eric Griego]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/third_democrat_vies_to_succeed_heinrich_in_nm_district-208213-1.html |title=Third Democrat Vies to Succeed Heinrich in N.M. District: Roll Call Politics |publisher=Rollcall.com |date=August 17, 2011 |access-date=September 14, 2012}}</ref> She defeated [[Janice Arnold-Jones]], a former member of the [[New Mexico House of Representatives]], in the November general election,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kob.com/article/stories/S2758906.shtml?cat=504|title=Congressional opponents face off in first debate|publisher=[[KOB]]|date=September 9, 2012|access-date=September 14, 2012|archive-date=September 1, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130901043359/http://www.kob.com/article/stories/S2758906.shtml?cat=504}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/decision2012/new-mexico-election-results-2012-heinrich-wins-senate-race-grisham-joins-her-cousin-lujan-in-congress-latino-population-lifts-obama-to-win/2012/11/07/c0978ec4-23a7-11e2-ac85-e669876c6a24_story.html|title=New Mexico Election Results 2012: Heinrich wins Senate race; Grisham joins her cousin Lujan in Congress; Latino population lifts Obama to win|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|date=November 7, 2012|access-date=November 7, 2012}}</ref> 59%–41%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nmtelegram.com/2012/11/07/lujan-grisham-blowout-could-mean-no-more-swing-congressional-districts/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131101233750/http://www.nmtelegram.com/2012/11/07/lujan-grisham-blowout-could-mean-no-more-swing-congressional-districts/|url-status=usurped|archive-date=November 1, 2013|title=Lujan Grisham blowout could mean no more swing Congressional districts|work=New Mexico Telegram|first=Matthew|last=Reichbach|date=November 7, 2012|access-date=November 10, 2012}}</ref>

==== 2014 ====
{{See also|2014 United States House of Representatives elections in New Mexico#District 1}}
[[File:Michelle Lujan Grisham official photo (cropped 2).jpg|thumb|Grisham during the [[113th United States Congress|113th Congress]]]]
Lujan Grisham defeated Republican Mike Frese in the 2014 elections, 59% to 41%.<ref>{{cite news |last=Ruiz |first=Regina |date=November 5, 2014 |title=Michelle Lujan Grisham re-elected as congresswoman |url=http://www.koat.com/politics/koat-prediction-grisham-reelected-as-congresswoman/29530392 |work=KOAT-TV |location=Albuquerque, NM}}</ref>

==== 2016 ====
{{See also|2016 United States House of Representatives elections in New Mexico#District 1}}
In 2016, Lujan Grisham defeated Republican Richard Priem, receiving 179,380 votes (65.1%) to Priem's 96,061 (34.9%).<ref>{{cite news |date=December 13, 2016 |title=New Mexico U.S. House 1st District Results: Michelle Lujan Grisham Wins |url=https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/new-mexico-house-district-1-lujan-grisham-priem |work=[[The New York Times]] |location=New York, NY}}</ref>

===Tenure===
Lujan Grisham was sworn in as a member of Congress on January 3, 2013. In 2016, she was one of nine members of Congress who took a trip to [[Baku]] who were later found to have been secretly funded by the government of [[Azerbaijan]]; she had to turn over gifts the country gave her to the House Clerk after an ethics investigation. Both the Office of Congressional Ethics and House Ethics Committee found lawmakers and aides had no way of knowing the trip was being funded improperly.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://thehill.com/business-a-lobbying/business-a-lobbying/283682-lawmakers-turned-over-gifts-after-trip/|title=Lawmakers turned over gifts after secretly funded trip to Azerbaijan|last=Dickson|first=Rebecca|date=June 16, 2016|website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|access-date=June 16, 2016}}</ref>

Also in 2016, Lujan Grisham was selected as the chair of the [[Congressional Hispanic Caucus]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/308416-hispanic-caucus-picks-new-leadership/|title=Hispanic Caucus picks new leadership|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|first=Rafael|last=Bernal|date=December 1, 2016|access-date=February 21, 2017}}</ref>

Lujan Grisham resigned her House seat as of December 31, 2018, to assume the governorship of New Mexico the following day.<ref>{{cite web|date=December 27, 2018|title=House Session|url=https://www.c-span.org/video/?456407-1/us-house-meets-day-6-government-shutdown|access-date=December 28, 2018|work=[[C-SPAN]]}}</ref>

