==Governor of New Mexico==
===Elections===

==== 2018 ====
{{see also|2018 New Mexico gubernatorial election}}
On December 13, 2016, one week after [[Tom Udall]] announced he would not run for [[governor of New Mexico]], Lujan Grisham became the first person to announce her candidacy to succeed [[Susana Martinez]], who was prohibited from running because of [[term limit]]s.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.abqjournal.com/908217/u-s-rep-michelle-lujan-grisham-announces-2018-gubernatorial-bid.html|title=Lujan Grisham running for governor|first=Dan|last=Boyd|work=Albuquerque Journal|date=December 13, 2016|access-date=December 14, 2016}}</ref> On June 5, 2018, she won the Democratic primary to become the party's nominee. On November 6, she was elected governor, defeating the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nominee, U.S. Representative [[Steve Pearce (politician)|Steve Pearce]], with 57.2% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kob.com/politics-news/democrat-michelle-lujan-grisham-to-become-new-mexicos-next-governor/5135681/|title=Despite technical difficulties, Lujan Grisham lays out vision for NM after winning race for Governor|date=2018-11-06|last=Panas|first=Joshua|publisher=KOB|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200514174747/https://www.kob.com/politics-news/democrat-michelle-lujan-grisham-to-become-new-mexicos-next-governor/5135681/ |archive-date=May 14, 2020 }}</ref>

==== 2022 ====
{{see also|2022 New Mexico gubernatorial election}}
On November 8, 2022, Lujan Grisham was reelected to a second term, defeating Republican nominee Mark Ronchetti with 52% of the vote.<ref name="GrishamRunning">{{cite web|date=June 3, 2021|title=Gov. Michelle Lujan Grisham will run for re-election|url=https://www.koat.com/article/gov-michelle-lujan-grisham-will-run-for-re-election/36623018|website=KOAT}}</ref>

===Tenure===
Lujan Grisham was sworn in on January 1, 2019.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.koat.com/article/governor-lujan-grisham-sworn-in/25718553 |title=Gov. Michelle Lujan Grisham promises sweeping change under her leadership |publisher=Koat.com |date=January 1, 2019 |access-date=June 30, 2019}}</ref> In September 2019, she announced a plan to make public universities in New Mexico tuition-free to state residents.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1367689/lujan-grisham-to-propose-free-college-for-all-new-mexicans.html|title=Governor pitches plan for free college|first=Ryan|last=Boetel|website=www.abqjournal.com}}</ref>

On January 29, 2019, Lujan Grisham signed an executive order calling for New Mexico to join the [[United States Climate Alliance]] and to reduce its [[greenhouse gas emissions]] by 45% below 2005 levels by 2030.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1274267/governor-signs-executive-order-on-climate-change.html|title=Governor signs executive order on climate change|last1=Boyd|first1=Dan|first2=Kevin |last2=Robinson-Avila|website=www.abqjournal.com|language=en-US|access-date=January 29, 2019}}</ref> This executive order also called for the state to develop comprehensive regulations to reduce [[methane]] emissions from the oil and gas sector, and for state agencies to work with the legislature to increase the state's [[Renewable portfolio standard (United States)|renewable portfolio standard]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.usclimatealliance.org/publications/2019/1/29/new-mexico-governor-michelle-lujan-grisham-joins-us-climate-alliance|title=New Mexico Governor Michelle Lujan Grisham Joins U.S. Climate Alliance|website=U.S. Climate Alliance|date=January 29, 2019 |language=en-US|access-date=January 29, 2019}}</ref>

In March 2019, Lujan Grisham signed New Mexico's Energy Transition Act. The legislation transitions the state's electricity sector away from coal and natural gas and toward a renewable economy, requiring New Mexico's electricity to be 50% renewable by 2030 and 100% from zero-carbon sources by 2045. She called the legislation "a promise to future generations of New Mexicans."<ref>{{Cite web|last1=Storrow|first1=Benjamin|date=March 13, 2019|title=Energy Transitions: Southwestern U.S.: from laggard to leader on climate|url=https://www.eenews.net/stories/1060127147|access-date=2020-07-01|website=www.eenews.net|language=en}}</ref>

On September 5, 2020, Lujan Grisham was named a co-chair of the Biden-Harris Transition Team, which was planning [[presidential transition of Joe Biden|Joe Biden's presidential transition]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Cindy McCain Joins Biden-Harris Transition Team's Advisory Board |url=https://buildbackbetter.com/press-releases/cindy-mccain-joins-biden-harris-transition-teams-advisory-board/ |website=President-Elect Joe Biden |access-date=November 9, 2020 |date=September 28, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Biden Transition Organization - Staff, Advisors |url=https://www.democracyinaction.us/2020/chrntran/bidentransition.html |website=www.democracyinaction.us |access-date=November 9, 2020 |date=November 9, 2017}}</ref> In November, Lujan Grisham was named a candidate for [[United States Secretary of Health and Human Services]] in the [[Biden administration]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Who Are Contenders for Biden's Cabinet? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/11/11/us/politics/biden-cabinet.html?action=click&module=Top%20Stories&pgtype=Homepage |access-date=November 11, 2020 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=November 11, 2020}}</ref> On December 3, 2020, she was elected chair of the [[Democratic Governors Association]] for 2021, having served as vice chair in 2020.<ref>{{cite web |title=Democratic Governors Select Gov. Michelle Lujan Grisham As Next DGA Chair |url=https://democraticgovernors.org/news/democratic-governors-select-gov-michelle-lujan-grisham-as-next-dga-chair/ |website=Democratic Governors Association |access-date=December 3, 2020}}</ref>

In May 2021, Lujan Grisham and 12 others were named as defendants in a lawsuit filed on behalf of the former executive director of the New Mexico Educational Retirement Board, alleging that she was not compensated at the same rate as her male counterparts.<ref>{{cite news|last1=McKay|first1=Dan|date=May 27, 2021|title=Suit accuses governor of discrimination involving pay|publisher=Albuquerque Journal|url=https://www.abqjournal.com/2394830/ex-erb-director-sues-lujan-grisham-over-salary.html|access-date=June 16, 2021}}</ref> In August 2021, Lujan Grisham signed an executive order joining Biden's "30x30" land goal.<ref>{{cite web | last1=Montoya Bryan | first1=Susan | title=New Mexico governor joins US conservation challenge | url=https://apnews.com/article/business-environment-and-nature-new-mexico-science--f6ffec2a72adf96f22a1d9ce193fc434 | date=August 25, 2021 | work=[[Associated Press]] | access-date=September 24, 2021}}</ref>

On September 8, 2023, Lujan Grisham issued an [[2023 Albuquerque gun ban|emergency order restricting carrying firearms in Albuquerque for 30 days]].<ref>{{Cite news |agency=Associated Press |date=2023-09-09 |title=New Mexico governor suspends right to carry firearms in public in Albuquerque |language=en-GB |work=[[The Guardian]] |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/09/new-mexico-governor-suspends-right-carry-firearms-albuquerque |access-date=2023-09-09 |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> The order has been criticized by Republicans, civil rights advocates, some Democrats, and gun safety advocates as unconstitutional.<ref>{{Cite news |author=Conor Powell |author2=Andy Rose |date=2023-09-09 |title=New Mexico governor orders suspension of open and concealed carry of guns in Albuquerque |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/09/09/us/new-mexico-gun-violence-emergency/index.html |access-date=2023-09-10 |work=[[CNN]]|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=davidhogg111/status/1700601787605266779 |url=https://twitter.com/davidhogg111/status/1700601787605266779 |access-date=2023-09-10 |website=X (formerly Twitter) |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Montoya Bryan |first1=Susan |title=Outrage intensifies over New Mexico governor's temporary gun ban as sheriff vows not to enforce it |url=https://apnews.com/article/albuquerque-guns-governor-concealed-carry-firearms-reject-ec5e8d57abc15687dd1b39d8cc197d96 |website=Associated Press News |date=September 11, 2023 |access-date=12 September 2023}}</ref> On September 13, Judge [[David H. Urias]] issued a [[temporary restraining order]] blocking her order until October 3, when a hearing was held. On October 3, Urias extended the preliminary hold while he considers blocking the restrictions indefinitely.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/news/judge-grants-temporary-restraining-order-against-gov-s-public-gun-ban/article_a44f00f2-527c-11ee-8dbc-53882e6249f4.html#3 |title=Judge grants temporary restraining order against gov.'s public gun ban |first=Oliver |last=Uyttebrouck |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]] |date=September 13, 2023 |access-date=September 14, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Lee |first1=Morgan |title=Court reviews gun-carry restrictions under health order in New Mexico, as states explore options |url=https://apnews.com/article/new-mexico-gun-suspension-lujan-grisham-8647d4e6569a8c8888768b3f26cb8b78 |access-date=4 October 2023 |work=AP News |date=3 October 2023 |language=en}}</ref> 

In April 2025, Lujan Grisham signed into law Senate Bill 16, which allows [[Unaffiliated voters|unaffiliated or independent voters]] to participate in major-[[Primary elections in the United States|party primaries]] without changing their registration. The reform, which was broadly opposed by [[New Mexico Republican Party|New Mexico Republicans]] and a few Democrats, takes effect in 2026 and aims to expand access for the state's growing number of independent voters (approximately 23% as of 2025), increasing voter participation and reducing [[partisan polarization]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-04-08 |title=New Mexico opens major-party primary elections to growing ranks of unaffiliated voters |url=https://apnews.com/article/new-mexico-open-primaries-87d4d04bf0de858f2287f1d36b360b4e |access-date=2025-04-30 |work=AP News |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Lohmann |first=Patrick |date=March 21, 2025 |title=NM House narrowly endorses open primaries for unaffiliated voters |url=https://sourcenm.com/briefs/nm-house-narrowly-endorses-open-primaries-for-unaffiliated-voters/ |access-date=2025-04-30 |website=Source New Mexico |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Wilham |first=T. J. |date=2025-04-09 |title=NM open primaries could lead to more moderate candidates, experts say |url=https://www.koat.com/article/new-mexico-open-primary-impact/64422806 |access-date=2025-04-30 |website=KOAT |language=en}}</ref>

== Political positions ==
[[File:-Handsoff Budget Rally (34485846020).jpg|thumb|Michelle Lujan Grisham speaking at a budget rally, May 2017]]

=== Abortion ===
{{See also|Abortion in New Mexico}}
Lujan Grisham supports [[Abortion-rights movement|abortion rights]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Thorp |first=Lauren |date=2022-06-27 |title=Gov. Lujan Grisham takes action to safeguard abortion access, protect New Mexico health care providers |url=https://www.governor.state.nm.us/2022/06/27/gov-lujan-grisham-takes-action-to-safeguard-abortion-access-protect-new-mexico-health-care-providers/ |access-date=2025-07-16 |website=Office of the Governor - Michelle Lujan Grisham |language=en-US}}</ref>

In 1969, the [[New Mexico Legislature]] passed a law that made it a felony for someone to provide a woman with an abortion unless it was needed to save a woman's life, or because her pregnancy was a result of rape or incest.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.lcsun-news.com/story/news/local/new-mexico/legislature/2019/03/15/new-mexico-senate-ban-abortion-law-criminal-prohibition-supreme-court/3173317002/|title=New Mexico Senate upholds dormant ban on abortion|website=Las Cruces Sun-News|language=en|access-date=2019-05-28}}</ref> The [[Supreme Court of the United States|U.S. Supreme Court]]'s decision in 1973's ''[[Roe v. Wade]]'' barred states from regulating abortion in the first trimester;<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Buell|first=Samuel|date=1991-01-01|title=Criminal Abortion Revisited|url=https://scholarship.law.duke.edu/faculty_scholarship/2174|journal=New York University Law Review|volume=66|issue=6|pages=1774–1831|pmid=11652642}}</ref> consequently, New Mexico's 1969 abortion law became unenforceable.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1254091/antiquated-nm-abortion-law-targeted-for-repeal.html|title=NM abortion law targeted for repeal|last=Nathanson |first=Rick |website=www.abqjournal.com|date=December 5, 2018}}</ref><ref name="auto">{{cite news|url=https://www.usnews.com/news/best-states/new-mexico/articles/2019-03-14/new-mexico-state-senate-upholds-dormant-ban-on-abortion|title=New Mexico State Senate Upholds Dormant Ban on Abortion|work=[[U.S. News & World Report]]|date=March 14, 2019|last=Lee|first=Morgan}}</ref>

In her 2019 State of the State address, Lujan Grisham expressed her support for repealing the 1969 law, saying, "The old criminal abortion law of this state, only one of nine left in the entire country, must go. Bring me that bill and I will sign it."<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kunm.org/post/lujan-grisham-vows-repeal-state-abortion-ban |title=Lujan Grisham Vows To Repeal State Abortion Ban |first=May |last=Ortega |website=www.kunm.org |date=January 16, 2019}}</ref> On March 3, 2019, she published an op-ed in support of repeal.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1287439/governor-will-remove-nms-ban-on-abortions.html|title=OPINION &#124; Governor will remove NM's ban on abortions|first=Michelle Lujan|last=Grisham|website=www.abqjournal.com}}</ref> Repeal legislation passed the New Mexico House of Representatives,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.lcsun-news.com/story/news/local/new-mexico/legislature/2019/02/07/new-mexico-house-votes-remove-criminal-abortion-ban/2800864002/|title=New Mexico House votes to rescind state abortion ban|website=Las Cruces Sun-News|date=February 7, 2019}}</ref> but was defeated in the State Senate on March 14, 2019, by a vote of 24–18.<ref name="auto" /> After the Senate vote, Lujan Grisham said, "This old, outdated statute criminalizing health care providers is an embarrassment. That removing it was even a debate, much less a difficult vote for some senators, is inexplicable to me."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://nmpoliticalreport.com/2019/03/15/senate-rejects-repealing-currently-unenforceable-anti-abortion-law/|title=Senate rejects repealing currently unenforceable anti-abortion law|first=Danielle|last=Prokop|date=March 15, 2019|website=The NM Political Report}}</ref>

In 2021, the New Mexico legislature passed SB10, a repeal of the 1969 abortion law. The bill was approved in the House by a 40–30 margin and in the Senate by a 25–17 margin. Lujan Grisham signed it into law on February 26, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2021 Regular Session - SB 10|url=https://www.nmlegis.gov/Legislation/Legislation?chamber=S&legtype=B&legno=10&year=21|access-date=June 3, 2021|website=New Mexico Legislature}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Nott|first=Robert|date=February 26, 2021|title=Governor signs bill repealing New Mexico abortion ban|work=Santa Fe New Mexican|url=https://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/legislature/governor-signs-bill-repealing-new-mexico-abortion-ban/article_5d197274-7860-11eb-9142-131136774e23.html|access-date=June 3, 2021}}</ref> As of June 2025, abortion is legal in New Mexico at all stages of pregnancy.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-06-28 |title=State Facts About Abortion: New Mexico |url=https://www.guttmacher.org/fact-sheet/state-facts-about-abortion-new-mexico |access-date=2023-05-24 |website=Guttmacher Institute |language=en}}</ref>

===Guns===
Lujan Grisham was a co-sponsor of the Assault Weapon Ban, H. R. 4269, introduced on December 12, 2015.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/bill/114th-congress/house-bill/4269/cosponsors|title=Cosponsors - H.R.4269 - 114th Congress (2015-2016): Assault Weapons Ban of 2015|first=David N.|last=Cicilline|date=January 15, 2016|website=www.congress.gov}}</ref>

=== Israel ===
Lujan Grisham is a strong supporter of [[Israel]]. She condemned the [[United Nations Security Council Resolution 2334|United Nations Security Council]]'s criticism of Israel's [[Israeli settlement|settlement building]] in the occupied [[Palestinian territories]].<ref>{{cite news |title=NM House members oppose Obama on Israel vote |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/923103/house-members-oppose-obama-on-israel.html |work=Albuquerque Journal |date=January 7, 2017}}</ref>

===Marijuana legalization===
In 2019, after a bill to [[Legalization of non-medical cannabis in the United States|legalize recreational marijuana]] passed the New Mexico House but not the Senate, Lujan Grisham announced that she would add the issue to the legislative agenda for the upcoming year.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McKay |first1=Dan |title=Recreational marijuana will be back in 2020 session |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1292850/recreational-marijuana-will-be-back-in-2020-session.html |access-date=June 1, 2021 |work=Albuquerque Journal |date=March 16, 2019}}</ref> She also announced the formation of a working group to determine the best path forward for legalization during the 2020 session.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Boyd |first1=Dan |title=Gov. Lujan Grisham creates cannabis legalization task force |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1334408/nm-governor-creates-group-to-study-cannabis-legalization.html |access-date=June 1, 2021 |work=Albuquerque Journal |date=June 28, 2019}}</ref> In 2021, after the legislature failed to legalize cannabis during the regular session, Lujan Grisham called a special session so that lawmakers could pass a legalization bill.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lee |first1=Morgan |title=Governor calls special legislative session in New Mexico |url=https://apnews.com/article/michelle-lujan-grisham-recreational-marijuana-marijuana-new-mexico-marijuana-legalization-bc5d57804928492938520c449f51ed80 |access-date=June 1, 2020 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=March 26, 2021}}</ref> She signed the bill into law on April 12, 2021.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Chacón |first1=Daniel J. |title=New Mexico legalizes recreational cannabis |url=https://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/local_news/new-mexico-legalizes-recreational-cannabis/article_5449fb02-9b95-11eb-8642-27a692a12f1e.html |access-date=June 1, 2021 |work=Santa Fe New Mexican |date=April 12, 2021}}</ref>

=== Minimum wage ===
In 2015, Lujan Grisham co-sponsored legislation that would raise the [[Minimum wage in the United States|federal minimum wage]] to $12/hour.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://nmpoliticalreport.com/3498/dems-in-congress-pushing-for-12-per-hour-minimum-wage/|title=Dems in Congress pushing for $12 per hour minimum wage {{!}} The NM Political Report|website=nmpoliticalreport.com|date=May 7, 2015|language=en-US|access-date=June 10, 2017}}</ref>

=== 2024 presidential election ===
After [[Joe Biden]] [[Withdrawal of Joe Biden from the 2024 United States presidential election|dropped out]] of the [[2024 United States presidential election|2024 presidential election]] and [[Kamala Harris]] became the front-runner for the Democratic nomination, Lujan Grisham endorsed Harris and said she was confident that Harris would win [[2024 United States presidential election in New Mexico|New Mexico]] in the [[United States Electoral College|Electoral College]], which she did, by six percentage points.<ref>{{Cite web |last=dchacon@sfnewmexican.com |first=Daniel J. Chacón |date=2024-07-22 |title=Lujan Grisham endorses Harris, says she's 'confident' she'll win New Mexico |url=https://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/local_news/lujan-grisham-endorses-harris-says-shes-confident-shell-win-new-mexico/article_7de5136c-4848-11ef-b12e-23230d652d8b.html |access-date=2024-08-13 |website=Santa Fe New Mexican |language=en}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Lujan Grisham married Gregory Grisham in 1982. They were married until his death from a [[Intracranial aneurysm|brain aneurysm]] in 2004 at the age of 45. The couple had two daughters.<ref name=abq2>{{cite web |last=Heild |first=Colleen |url=http://www.abqjournal.com/news/metro/271788metro12-23-07.htm |title=ABQjournal Metro: Doctor Says Dropped Suit Hurt Reputation |publisher=Abqjournal.com |date=December 23, 2007 |access-date=September 14, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131103022009/http://www.abqjournal.com/news/metro/271788metro12-23-07.htm |archive-date=November 3, 2013 }}</ref> Lujan Grisham filed a wrongful death suit against her husband's physician,<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.bizjournals.com/albuquerque/stories/2007/07/16/daily23.html |title=Former health secretary files wrongful death suit – New Mexico Business Weekly |newspaper=Albuquerque Business First |publisher=Bizjournals.com |access-date=September 14, 2012}}</ref> but the lawsuit was dropped.<ref name=abq2/>

Lujan Grisham married Manuel Cordova, a small business owner from [[Albuquerque, New Mexico|Albuquerque]], on May 21, 2022, with [[Vice President of the United States]] [[Kamala Harris]] officiating the wedding. The wedding was postponed due to [[COVID-19]] restrictions.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.abqjournal.com/2490277/lujan-grisham-to-marry-longtime-fiance-manuel-cordova-next-month.html|title=VP Kamala Harris set to officiate Lujan Grisham's wedding next month|date=April 19, 2022|publisher=[[Albuquerque Journal]]|first=Dan|last=Boyd}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Lizza |first1=Ryan |last2=Bade |first2=Rachael |last3=Daniels |first3=Eugene |title=Playbook |url=http://politi.co/2A2M52A |access-date=2022-05-23 |website=[[Politico]]|language=en}}</ref> The couple had been together since 2012.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.abqjournal.com/2490277/lujan-grisham-to-marry-longtime-fiance-manuel-cordova-next-month.html | title=VP Kamala Harris set to officiate Lujan Grisham's wedding next month - Albuquerque Journal | date=April 19, 2022 }}</ref>

== Sexual battery settlement ==
In December 2019, a former campaign staffer and spokesperson, James Hallinan, accused Lujan Grisham of sexual battery. According to Hallinan, the incident took place during a staff meeting in 2018. By his account, Lujan Grisham poured a bottle of water on his crotch and then slapped and grabbed his crotch through his pants while laughing. He said the incident happened "in front of everybody".<ref name="nbcnews-grisham-settles">{{cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/new-mexico-governor-pays-62-500-former-staffer-sexual-harassment-n1264218 |title=New Mexico governor pays $62,500 to former staffer in sexual harassment settlement |first=Elisha|last=Fieldstadt |publisher=[[NBC News]] |date=April 15, 2021 |access-date=April 15, 2021}}</ref>

In April 2021, it was publicized that Lujan Grisham and her gubernatorial campaign, while denying the allegations, had reached a $62,500 settlement with the law firm representing Hallinan. The payment was made in monthly installments of $12,500 from November 2020 to March 2021.<ref name="abqj-grisham-settles">{{cite news|last1=McKay|first1=Dan|last2=Boyd|first2=Dan|date=April 12, 2021|title=Gov.'s campaign settles with ex-spokesman|publisher=[[Albuquerque Journal]]|url=https://www.abqjournal.com/2379452/gov-s-campaign-settles-with-ex-spokesman.html}}</ref> Her political committee paid another $87,500 over six months, bringing the total payout to $150,000.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.usnews.com/news/best-states/new-mexico/articles/2021-10-11/new-mexico-governor-settles-harassment-claim-for-150k|title=New Mexico Governor Settles Harassment Claim for $150K|date=2021-10-11|publisher=[[U.S. News & World Report]]|access-date=2023-04-08}}</ref>

== Ethics controversy ==
In February 2021, an investigative team from Albuquerque TV station [[KOB (TV)|KOB]] found reports of more than $6,500 worth of groceries bought from the governor's contingency fund, which is intended for travel or official functions to promote the state. According to public receipts through an Inspection of Public Records Act request, the items include anything from "laundry detergent to Wagyu beef, tuna steaks, top sirloin and hundreds of dollars in alcohol purchases."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ramirez |first1=Chris |title=4 Investigates: Review of expenses reveals governor spending most of her discretionary fund on groceries |url=https://www.kob.com/albuquerque-news/4-investigates-review-of-expenses-reveals-governor-spending-most-of-her-discretionary-fund-on-groceries/6016437/ |website=kob.com |date=February 18, 2021 |access-date=6 July 2021}}</ref>

